import TpuSyncVerify.Common.System
import TpuSyncVerify.Common.ModelCheck

/-!
# A grouped layer's host layer buffer under bounded host layer staging

With fewer host layer buffers than layers (`TPU_RAIDEN_NUM_STAGING_LAYERS`), a
send leases one buffer per layer (`StagingLayerManager`) and copies the layer
into it before pushing it. With push groups, a layer that moves in several
units copies and pushes each unit separately, and all of them use the layer's
one buffer: `AcquireStagingSlot(unit.layer)` leases it for the first unit and
returns the same lease for the others. This model is that buffer's life, for
one layer of `numUnits` units: the buffer goes back to the pool only when the
layer's last unit has been pushed (`--unpushed_units_[unit.layer] == 0` in the
push callback), or once the send is draining.

Abbreviations: `send.cc` = `tpu_sync/kv_cache/transfer_send_session.cc`,
`staging.cc` = `tpu_sync/kv_cache/staging_manager.cc`. Exact line ranges for
every symbol and sub-block are in the generated `## Source map` table below.

## What is modelled

`LayerStaging.lean` proves the bounded host layer staging protocol with one
push per layer, releasing a layer's buffer when that push completes. With push
groups the layer's release moves to its last unit's push; this model shows
that this keeps the premise `LayerStaging.lean` relies on: a buffer that
returns to the pool while the send is not draining holds nothing that a unit
of its layer still needs. Without push groups a layer has one unit and the two
rules coincide. The units of a layer cover disjoint blocks of its buffer (each
copies `host_block_ids[unit.begin, unit.end)`), so one unit's copy never writes
what another unit's push reads; the hazard is another layer's copy, after the
buffer went back to the pool.

## State

| Field | C++ | Role |
|---|---|---|
| `numUnits` | the layer's units (`unpushed_units_[l] = groups.size();`) | |
| `draining` | `draining_` (sticky) | |
| `held` | `StagingLayerManager::leases_` holds the layer | |
| `staged` | ghost | units whose D2H copy wrote the buffer |
| `pushing` | ghost | unit pushes in flight, reading the buffer |
| `pushedOk` | `numUnits - unpushed_units_[layer]` | units pushed OK |
| `gone` | ghost | staged units whose push failed or that were dropped |
| `clobbered` | ghost | another layer's copy wrote the buffer while a unit still needed it |

## Events

| Event | C++ |
|---|---|
| `d2h` | the D2H loop's iteration for the layer's next unit in `StartPush`: `AcquireStagingSlot(unit.layer)` (refused once draining), `if (draining_)`, then `D2hSyncDispatch` into the buffer |
| `push` | `IssuePush` issuing a staged unit |
| `pushDone ok` | the push callback: on success `release_layer = --self->unpushed_units_[unit.layer] == 0`, then `Release(unit.layer)` if so; on error `FinishLocked` and `Release(unit.layer)` |
| `drop` | a staged unit dropped because the send is draining: `SendNextUnit`'s callback, `AdmitPush` or `IssuePush` finding `draining_`, each with `Release(layer)` |
| `cancel` | `Finish(error)` |
| `reuse` | another layer's `AcquireStagingSlot` takes the free buffer and its copy writes it (A1) |

## Lock scopes

`StagingLayerManager` serializes leases under its own `mu_`; the counters are
the session's, under the session's `mu_`. The push callback decides
`release_layer` under the session's lock and calls `Release` after it; nothing
in between can lease the buffer to another layer before the release, so the
events are atomic.

## Assumptions

* **A1 (no copy once draining).** A layer's copy into a buffer happens only
  while the send is not draining: `AcquireStagingSlot` fails once `draining_`
  (and `FinishLocked` shuts the manager down, `staging_layer_manager_.Shutdown()`
  with `cancelled_`), and the D2H loop re-checks `if (draining_)` after
  acquiring. Encoded as `reuse` requiring `draining = false`. This is why a
  failed push may release a buffer that other units of its layer are still
  reading.

## Properties

* **`Safe`** — no other layer's copy writes the buffer while a unit of this
  layer is waiting for its push or being pushed from it.

`reachable_safe` holds for any number of units. The mutant
`stepReleaseEachPush` (every OK push releases the buffer, the one-push-per-layer
rule) lets the next layer overwrite a unit still waiting for its push.
`TransferSendSessionTest.GroupedLayerKeepsItsHostBufferUntilItsLastPush` checks
the same in C++.

